<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#attrition">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Attrition</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Attrition refers to a form of warfare in which victory depends on wearing down the enemy's manpower and material resources through continuous losses. Such wars favour states with larger populations and industries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> suffered from attrition in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> was exhausted by it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> lost a war of attrition against American industrial power in the Pacific.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The multinational army was unsuited to the demands of industrial total war: its logistics were overwhelmed, its reserves of trained officers were destroyed in the first campaigns, and its command struggled to maintain cohesion across many linguistic communities under sustained <b>attrition</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Battle of Midway in June 1942, which cost Japan four fleet carriers, and the Guadalcanal campaign of August 1942 to February 1943 surrendered the strategic initiative to the United States for good, exposing a military designed for swift conquest rather than sustained industrial <b>attrition</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1001 Basil II waged a long war of <b>attrition</b>, taking fortress after fortress.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>A strategic culture built on aggressive offensive warfare, superbly effective against conventional opponents, proved unable to answer the Fabian strategy of scorched-earth retreat and <b>attrition</b> that Peter the Great employed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1039–40 Jingzong besieged Yanzhou with over 100,000 men and annihilated Ren Fu's 30,000 at Haoshuichuan, yet took no territory from forts held by 200,000 rotating Song troops; half his soldiers died of <b>attrition</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1969 it inspired Naomi Shemer's &quot;Shivchei Ma'oz&quot;, performed by the Israel Defense Forces' Southern Command Band, which tied the old hymn to the positions of the Bar Lev Line, then under attack in the War of <b>Attrition</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/maoz-tzur">Ma'oz Tzur</a></li></ul></section>
